<P>With a wave of his hand, he once again quieted the glade. "Please, quiet…Also, I’d like to take a bit more of your time to commend one of our initaites for his exceptional valor, and honor, in battle."</P><br><P>Master Sureshot motioned to the crowd and a druid stepped forth holding a long, narrow box of polished oak. He took the box and turned to me, opening it toward me, so that I could see its contents. I glanced back and forth, wondering what he was doing…or rather, who he was looking at.</P><br><P>"Eshkin kept his head in the crisis. He pulled the party together and hatched the plan that saved us. He fought with courage and honor--both to his friends and his enemies."</P><br><P>Next to me, I heard Kartum slowly exhaling a hissing breath of suppressed malice through his teeth.</P><br><P>"…Eshkin, I would like to present you with this arrow. Carry it with you always, and it will never fail you."</P><br><P>If I was thinking about Kartum and his jealous reaction at that moment, it was quickly dispelled at the sight of the arrow. Its head of forged of steel blades and its vanes were of bone. The shaft was of darkwood; that same material of the magic bow, I had seen so many years ago.</P><br><P>"Uh…Master," I said, "This really not necessary. I only did what I could…"</P><br><P>"Nonsense, Eshkin…"</P><br><P>"No, really sir, anyone would have…"</P><br><P>"ESHKIN!" he yelled suddenly.</P><br><P>"Uh…yes, sir?"</P><br><P>"Shut up."</P><br><P>"Aye. Yes, sir." I took the arrow and placed it in my quiver.</P><br><P>The air of the glade was filed with wild clapping and cheering.</P><br><P>It was the proudest moment in my life…back from a day when I could feel pride.</P><br><P>.</P><br><P>My trade as a hunter was, from that day, touched with a blessing from Tunare. My skills solidified and no quarry seemed able to elude me. When I went into the hills to hunt, I suddenly found myself in need of other intitiates to help me carry the game back to the Glade. My pelts wre always of the best quality and brought good prices form the merchants of Qeynos. Even the gnolls of Blackburrow seemed to vanish from sight for a time.</P><br><P>My father was getting old now, and expressed a desire to leave off the smithing business. One day, in the sumertime, he called me back to the smithy.</P><br><P>"Eshkin, I will be straight with you, you have made a good name for yourself as a hunter. I think that by the next harvests, Hager Sureshot will declare you a Ranger."</P><br><P>I poured my father another mug of coffee and seated myself at the table. "Thank you, father. That is my hope."</P><br><P>"You have done well, and I have raised you right. I raised you to never lie. So you must tell me something now, in perfect truth."</P><br><P>"Of course, father."</P><br><P>"Do you have any desire to keep up the smithy, after I am gone?"</P><br><P>This was nothing like the question I had been expecting. I am not certain what I was expecting, but that was not it. "Father?"</P><br><P>"Do you wish to smith here in the village, or hunt with the Jaggedpine Treefolk?’</P><br><P>It was not a hard decision, "I hope I do not offend, father, but I am a hunter now. I wish to continue with that."</P><br><P>He was not surprised, "Very well. I ask it because I am quitting. I will travel next spring to the Karanas to live with my sister…permanently."</P><br><P>I paused for a moment. He was trying to tell me something, but I was not sure what. "What of the smithy, father?"</P><br><P>"It is yours, son. Take it. Do with it what you wish. If you do not wish to smith, you can get a very handsome offer on it from the merchants in Qeynos. It will be good money for you."</P><br><P>"But what about you?"</P><br><P>He laughed. It was a rare and welcome laugh; one I had not heard in a long time. "I will be fine, son. You don’t think I have been smithing all these years without squirrling some plat away, do you?"</P><br><P>So that was that.</P><br><P>My father left in the spring for the Karanas and the smithy was empty. He left with me the rolled parchement of title that declared the smithy mine.</P><br><P></P><br><CENTER>***</CENTER>         <br>
